>wait
Time passes.

"Hub Station," says the pilot. "Mind the gap." There is no gap. You mind it
anyway.

Hub Station: Arrivals
A long, low arrivals hall under a ceiling of strip lights, with the sort of
carpet that was chosen to hide things. A kiosk stands in the middle of the
floor; a plant, a very old plant, waits in a pot beside it. A sign says QUEUE
HERE and points at the floor. Customs is to the east.

>consult guide about the mish mash
WSOGMM: THE WHOLE SORT OF GENERAL MISH MASH. THE GUIDE'S PREFERRED NAME FOR THE
FACT THAT EVERYTHING THAT MIGHT HAVE HAPPENED IS STILL LYING ABOUT IN ONE HEAP,
LIKE A WARDROBE AFTER A HOLIDAY. EVERY EARTH THERE HAS EVER BEEN IS IN IT. SO IS
YOUR LUNCH. THE GUIDE CAN TIDY IT. THE GUIDE WOULD ASK YOU NOT TO WORRY ABOUT
WHERE IT PUTS THINGS.

[Out of the story: the joke has a respectable ancestor. In 1957 the physicist
Hugh Everett III published a paper on the "relative state" formulation of
quantum mechanics, from which grew the many-worlds interpretation, in which
every possible outcome of a quantum event is real in some branch of a larger
whole. Richard Feynman's sum over histories (1948) likewise treats a particle as
taking every path at once. Neither would care to be quoted on sandwiches. The
Mish Mash is the game having a little fun at their expense.]

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

